    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d425">

                <pb facs="0616" n="516" sameAs="frus:frus1934v01/pg516"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Mutual Guarantee (Eastern Locarno)/56:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-09-25T21:00:00+03:00">September 25,
                            1934—9 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 26—4 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>325. My No. 320, September 24.<anchor n="44" xml:id="d425-f1"/> Rubinin<anchor
                        n="45" xml:id="d425-f2"/> informed me today that the Soviet Government had
                    given up all hope that the Eastern Locarno agreement might be pushed through. He
                    added that the Soviet Government was still confident that France and
                    Czechoslovakia would make an agreement with the Soviet Union for mutual defense.
                    He said that the Soviet Government was fully aware that Great Britain and Italy
                    were bringing great pressure to bear on France to refuse to make any definite
                    agreement but that he believed the French had no intention of turning from their
                    present course. The French Ambassador<anchor n="46" xml:id="d425-f3"/> last
                    night assured me that there was no chance whatever that France would yield to
                    British and Italian pressure. He stated that as soon as the defensive agreement
                    had been signed it would be made public. Both Rubinin and the French Ambassador
                    said that no word had yet been put on paper.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
